# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Behavioral Biometric Authentication — example environment configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in REAL values there. `.env` is git-ignored; this
# file ships only placeholders and NEVER a real secret.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Application identity -----------------------------------------------------
BBA_APP_NAME=Behavioral Biometric Authentication API
BBA_APP_VERSION=0.1.0
BBA_ENVIRONMENT=development
BBA_DEBUG=false
BBA_API_PREFIX=/api/v1

# CORS: explicit origins only (never "*").
# NOTE: the single BBA_CORS_ORIGINS definition lives in the container/production
# section further down, because the deployed backend is the authoritative one.
# Do not add a second definition — docker compose takes the LAST value in this
# file, so a duplicate earlier here would be silently ignored.
# For host-local, non-container development set it to http://localhost:5173.

# ML artifacts (Phase 7 checkpoint + Phase 9B inference artifacts) ---------
BBA_CHECKPOINT_PATH=models/siamese_behavioral_encoder.pt
BBA_PREPROCESSING_ARTIFACT_PATH=models/behavioral_preprocessing.json
BBA_VERIFICATION_CONFIG_PATH=models/verification_config.json

# PostgreSQL (Phase 10/11: profiles + accounts) ----------------------------
# Leave empty to use the non-persistent in-memory stores (dev/tests only).
# BBA_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/behavioral

# Authentication (Phase 11) -------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED: replace with a long random secret (>= 32 characters) before any
# auth endpoint can issue tokens. Generate one with e.g.:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
# There is deliberately NO default secret — an empty value makes auth fail
# closed (503 auth_unavailable) instead of signing with a weak key.
BBA_JWT_SECRET_KEY=
BBA_JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
BBA_JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Container deployment (Phase 15A/B — docker compose) — ALSO read from `.env`.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   scripts/generate_dev_certs.sh      # one-time: local dev TLS certs
#   docker compose up --build -d
#
# compose.yaml reads the variables below from `.env` (git-ignored) and passes
# them to the services. NEVER commit real values.
#
# Start a `.env` that composes cleanly:
#   1. Generate a real JWT secret and set BBA_JWT_SECRET_KEY below.
#      Leaving it empty makes `docker compose up` fail with a clear error
#      (fail closed) rather than booting with a weak secret.
#   2. Set BBA_CORS_ORIGINS to the BROWSER origin (with the TLS proxy it is
#      the HTTPS origin, https://localhost by default — NOT :3000 anymore).
#   3. Replace POSTGRES_PASSWORD with a random value (hex or URL-safe so the
#      constructed BBA_DATABASE_URL does not need URL-encoding), e.g.:
#        python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
#   4. Run scripts/generate_dev_certs.sh (idempotent) so the proxy has TLS
#      certificates; BBA_TLS_CERT_PATH/BBA_TLS_KEY_PATH below may point them
#      elsewhere when mounting operator-provided (trusted) certs.
#
# BBA_ENVIRONMENT is pinned to `production` (TLS, metric collection on) by
# compose.yaml internally, so the `development` value above only ever applies
# to non-container workflows.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL (deployed backend) ----------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_USER=bba
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-random-hex-64-chars
POSTGRES_DB=behavioral

# Deployed backend -----------------------------------------------------------
# BBA_JWT_SECRET_KEY is read from the section above; set it to a real secret.
# CORS must allow the deployed BROWSER origin. With the TLS reverse proxy
# (Phase 15B) the browser only ever talks to the proxy on HTTPS, so this MUST
# be the HTTPS origin (https://localhost by default) — NOT the old :3000.
BBA_CORS_ORIGINS=https://localhost

# Structured JSON request logging (backend, Phase 15B).
# DEFAULT: INFO. Valid: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL, NOTSET.
BBA_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Browser-facing API base URL, baked into the frontend bundle at build time
# (VITE_API_BASE_URL -> src/api.js). With the TLS proxy the browser and the
# API share the single HTTPS origin, so this is a SAME-ORIGIN relative path.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api/v1

# Reverse proxy (Phase 15B — the ONLY service publishing host ports) ----------
HTTP_PORT=80
HTTPS_PORT=443

# TLS certificate/key mounted INTO the proxy container (read-only).
# Defaults to the local development certs generated by
# scripts/generate_dev_certs.sh. Override to mount a trusted certificate.
BBA_TLS_CERT_PATH=./proxy/certs/server.crt
BBA_TLS_KEY_PATH=./proxy/certs/server.key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Oracle Cloud production deployment (Phase D2)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The stack is deployed with the SAME compose.yaml used for local testing — the
# only difference is the values you set in `.env` on the VM. No separate
# production compose file exists, and none is needed.
#
# Target:  Oracle Ampere A1, minimum 4 GB RAM.
#          (The 1 GB AMD micro is too small: the backend image build needs
#           ~1.5-2 GB RAM for the CPU PyTorch install.)
#
# Network: only TCP 80 and TCP 443 need public ingress. The proxy is the ONLY
#          service that publishes host ports; PostgreSQL, the backend and the
#          frontend are reachable only on the internal Compose network.
#          Restrict SSH to the administrator's IP address where possible.
#
# 1. Create `.env` on the VM (it is git-ignored; never commit it):
#       cp .env.example .env
#
# 2. Generate the two secrets (run these ON THE VM; do not paste the output
#    into this file or anywhere in Git):
#       python3 -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"   # BBA_JWT_SECRET_KEY
#       python3 -c "import secrets;print(secrets.token_hex(32))"       # POSTGRES_PASSWORD
#
# 3. Set these values in `.env`:
#       BBA_CORS_ORIGINS=https://<PUBLIC_ORIGIN>
#       VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api/v1
#    BBA_CORS_ORIGINS must be the exact HTTPS origin the browser uses, with no
#    trailing slash. Keep VITE_API_BASE_URL as the relative /api/v1 so the
#    browser and API share one same-origin path through the proxy.
#
# 4. Lock the file down and validate before starting anything:
#       chmod 600 .env
#       docker compose config --quiet
#    `docker compose config` FAILS (non-zero exit) while any required secret is
#    missing — that is intentional fail-closed behaviour, not an error to work
#    around. It prints no secret values.
#
# ML ARTIFACTS — NOTHING TO DO:
#   models/siamese_behavioral_encoder.pt
#   models/behavioral_preprocessing.json
#   models/verification_config.json
#   These three FROZEN artifacts are already tracked in Git, so `git clone`
#   delivers them. Do NOT retrain, regenerate, re-export, or manually copy
#   them onto the VM. compose.yaml mounts ./models read-only at /app/models,
#   and BBA_CHECKPOINT_PATH / BBA_PREPROCESSING_ARTIFACT_PATH /
#   BBA_VERIFICATION_CONFIG_PATH are overwritten by compose.yaml with the
#   in-container /app/models/... paths, so the values in this file are only
#   used for host-local (non-container) runs.
#
# TLS — DEVELOPMENT CERIFICATES ONLY:
#   The certificates in proxy/certs/ are DEVELOPMENT certificates for
#   localhost (CN=localhost, SAN DNS:localhost, IP:127.0.0.1) produced by
#   scripts/generate_dev_certs.sh. They are NOT trusted by browsers and MUST
#   NOT be treated as production certificates for a real public hostname.
#   A later domain/TLS phase replaces them with a trusted certificate by
#   overriding BBA_TLS_CERT_PATH / BBA_TLS_KEY_PATH above; the keys are never
#   committed (.gitignore excludes *.key / *.pem / *.crt).
#
# Phase 16A is unaffected by any of this: deployment does not change the
# local-only export workflow, and raw sessions are still never uploaded
# automatically. See README.md — "DO NOT click 'Send Sessions to Server'
# for Phase 16A research collection".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
